.\" Manual page for detctl.  See the comment at the top of detc.8 for why these
.\" are roff and not markdown put through a converter.
.TH DETCTL 8 2026\-08\-04 "detc 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.
.SH NAME
detctl \- run a detc subcommand on other machines
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B detctl
.BI \-\-host " HOST"
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I SUBCOMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.br
.B detctl
.BI \-\-command " COMMAND"
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I SUBCOMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B detctl
takes the same subcommands as
.BR detc (8),
starts
.BR detcd (8)
on the far side, sends the call, and prints what comes back exactly as
.B detc
would have printed it there.
.PP
Nothing is installed on the machine that answers beyond the package itself, and
nothing runs there between two calls.  This program knows nothing about SSH
beyond how to build an argv, so a port, a user, a key or a jump host is what
.BR ssh_config (5)
is for and there is no second place to write them down.
.PP
Several machines are reached at once, each answering in a block of its own, in
the order they were asked, while the terminal is told on the standard error how
the run is going.  One machine is the way it has always been: the answers
arrive as they are spoken, the standard error of the child is the one of the
run, and the exit status is the one of whatever was started.
.PP
It is not the only client.
.BR varlinkctl (1)
reaches the same service and is better at showing the interface; what
.B detctl
adds is the output of
.B detc
instead of JSON, the fleet, and the exit status of the run.
.
.SS Naming a fleet
.B \-\-host
takes a host, several separated by commas, or any of the following, and the run
is everything they add up to:
.TP
.I name
A host, reached as
.BR ssh (1)
would reach it.
.TP
.I group
A group of the inventory, which may name other groups.
.TP
.I pattern
A shell pattern, such as
.IR stage\-web* .
It selects among the hosts the inventory names and never introduces one.
.TP
.IR name [ a : b ]
A range, counted out:
.I lab[01:12]
is
.I lab01
to
.IR lab12 ,
machines that are written down nowhere.  It counts letters as well as numbers
.RI ( rack[a:f] ),
and counts an address like a name
.RI ( 192.168.1.[10:250] ).
The two ends are the whole of what it reaches, so a run is the size it was
typed as and never the size the network happens to have.
.TP
.BI ! name
Takes one back out, whatever put it in.
.PP
.B \-\-command
names a machine that is not reached over SSH at all, by giving the shell
command that starts
.B detcd
there.  Both may be given, and then the run is their union.
.
.SS The inventory
The groups live in one YAML file belonging to whoever is typing:
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/detc/hosts.yaml
.RI ( ~/.config/detc/hosts.yaml
when that is unset), or wherever
.B \-\-inventory
or
.B DETC_HOSTS
says.
.PP
.EX
dmz:
  \- web1
  \- web2.example
stage:
  \- stage\-web1
  \- stage\-web2
lab:
  \- lab[01:12]     # a run of machines, counted out
web:
  \- dmz            # a group may name another
  \- stage\-web*     # and a pattern gathers the ones named above
.EE
.PP
It says which hosts there are and nothing about how to reach them.
.
.SH OPTIONS
Global, so they go before the subcommand.  One of
.B \-\-host
and
.B \-\-command
is required.
.TP
.BI \-\-host " HOST"
The machines to reach, as described above.  Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-command " COMMAND"
A shell command that starts
.B detcd
on something that is not a plain host, such as
.RB \(lq "podman exec \-i web /usr/bin/detcd" \(rq.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-inventory " FILE"
Read the groups from
.I FILE
instead of the one belonging to the user.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote\-path " PATH"
Where
.B detcd
is on the far side.
.I /usr/bin/detcd
by default.
.TP
.B \-\-sudo
Start
.B detcd
through
.BR sudo (8),
for a fleet reached as a user who is not root.
.TP
.BI \-o " OPTION"
An option for
.BR ssh (1),
passed as its own
.B \-o
would take it.  Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs " " \fIN\fR
How many machines to reach at a time, or
.I 0
for all of them at once.  Ten by default.  It bounds the
.B ssh
processes of one run and not the size of a fleet.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-progress
Do not report on the standard error how the run is going.  It is reported only
to a terminal in any case.
.TP
.BR \-\-watch [ =\fISECONDS\fR ]
Run the command again every
.I SECONDS
\(em sixty when the number is left out \(em and print a run only when it is not
the one before it.  A fleet that is converged therefore says nothing, and a
block appearing is a machine that moved.
.IP
The
.B =
is required, because the option is followed by a subcommand and there would
otherwise be no telling the two apart.
.IP
This is the one case where a run of one machine does not stream: nothing can be
printed until it is known whether it changed.
.TP
.BI \-\-watch\-count " N"
Stop after
.I N
runs of
.B \-\-watch
instead of running until it is interrupted.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Say what would happen instead of doing it, on every machine of the run.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Turn logging on, and repeat for more of it.  It is about this side of the
conversation; for the other side, pass
.B \-d
to
.B detcd
with
.BR \-\-remote\-path .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.
.SH COMMANDS
The same as
.BR detc (8),
with the same options:
.BR apply ,
.BR list ,
.BR cat ,
.BR check ,
.BR doc ,
.BR schema ,
.BR var ,
.B bundle
and
.BR report .
.PP
Paths in them are paths on the machine that answers, and the namespace a
.B \-k
or a
.B \-\-kv
overrides is the namespace of that machine.  The one thing that travels is a
bundle: what
.RB \(lq "detctl \-\-host web bundle install fleet.detc" \(rq
sends is the file, and what admits it is the
.I allowed_signers
of each machine that installs it.
.PP
There is no way to ask for another root.  Which tree is configured is decided
by whoever starts
.B detcd
on the far side.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
For one machine, the status of the run there \(em so a
.B check
that failed on that host fails here, and
.BR 255 ,
which is how
.B ssh
says the host could not be reached at all, comes through as itself.
.PP
For a fleet, the status is
.B 0
when every machine answered without failing and
.B 1
when any of them did not.  The blocks say which.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DETC_HOSTS
The inventory to read, for a run that is not driven from the home directory of
anybody.
.B \-\-inventory
overrides it.
.TP
.BR DETC_LOG_LEVEL ", " DETC_LOG_STYLE
As in
.BR detc (8),
and about this side only.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/detc/hosts.yaml
The inventory.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/config
Everything about how a host is reached, which this program deliberately does
not duplicate.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask a fleet what it would change, leaving one machine out:
.PP
.EX
$ detctl \-\-host web,'!web3' \-\-dry\-run apply
.EE
.PP
Watch a fleet until it is quiet, printing only the machines that move:
.PP
.EX
$ detctl \-\-host web \-\-watch=30 check
.EE
.PP
Send one bundle to everything in the inventory, keeping it across a reboot:
.PP
.EX
$ detctl \-\-host '*' bundle install fleet.detc \-\-persist \-\-apply
.EE
.PP
Reach something that is not a host:
.PP
.EX
$ detctl \-\-command "podman exec \-i web /usr/bin/detcd" list
.EE
.PP
Drive a fleet as an unprivileged user, twenty at a time:
.PP
.EX
$ detctl \-\-host lab[01:12] \-\-sudo \-j 20 \-o ConnectTimeout=5 apply
.EE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR detc (8),
.BR detcd (8),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR varlinkctl (1)
